<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludowingians</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century–1247</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Ludowingian dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ludowingians were a German noble dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> as landgraves from 1131 and also held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-duke-of-bavaria">Louis the Bearded</a> in the 11th century, they built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> and made their court a centre of courtly poetry. Landgrave Louis IV married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-hungary">Saint Elizabeth of Hungary</a>, and the line died out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-raspe">Henry Raspe</a> in 1247, after which Thuringia passed to the Wettins.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludowingians.jpg" alt="Ludowingians" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COA_family_de_Landgrafen_von_Hessen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
